@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+---
+title: Statistics and refresh rate
+description: Read playback diagnostics, evaluate thermal load, and match the display refresh rate to video FPS.
+---
+
+The diagnostics overlay is the quickest way to separate source, network, decoder, renderer, memory, and thermal problems.
+
+## What the statistics page shows
+
+Depending on the active source and device, the detailed page reports:
+
+- Media title, video output, video codec, and audio codec
+- Display FPS, source FPS, dropped frames, and decoder efficiency
+- Process CPU use and memory
+- Playback cache and network or torrent activity
+- Battery percentage, charge/discharge state, power, and temperature
+- HDR output state and selected HDR mode
+- Active play time, peak temperature, temperature rise, and thermal state
+
+Values can be unavailable when Android, the decoder, or the source does not expose them. Compare trends over several minutes rather than treating one sample as a benchmark.
+
+## Diagnose a playback problem
+
+| Symptom | First signals to compare |
+| --- | --- |
+| Periodic stalls | Cache duration, network activity, and source type |
+| Smooth audio but late video | Decoder, source FPS, display FPS, dropped frames |
+| Degrades after several minutes | Battery temperature, thermal state, CPU, shader load |
+| High power with smooth playback | Renderer, HDR conversion, Anime4K, visualizers, brightness |
+| Only one online source fails | Network throughput and extractor/server behavior |
+
+## Dynamic refresh rate
+
+On Android 11 and newer, mpvRx can request the current container frame rate from the playback surface. A compatible display may switch to a matching or related mode, such as 24, 30, or 60 Hz.
+
+The final choice remains with Android and the display. Mode switching can cause a brief blanking event, and some TVs or vendor ROMs ignore the request.
+
+:::tip
+Use refresh-rate matching for visible judder, not as a general performance switch. Disable it temporarily when diagnosing black flashes, HDMI resyncs, or unstable vendor display behavior.
+:::
+
+## Thermal interpretation
+
+Thermal headroom ranges from cool/full budget toward the device limit. mpvRx can reduce ambient shader samples as headroom falls and throttle Anime4K below severe thresholds. See [Performance and battery](/docs/reference/performance-and-battery) for a repeatable low-power baseline.
diff --git a/website/content/docs/playback/video-hdr-and-shaders.mdx b/website/content/docs/playback/video-hdr-and-shaders.mdx
index 9dd242f0..5d9c5bc2 100644
--- a/website/content/docs/playback/video-hdr-and-shaders.mdx
+++ b/website/content/docs/playback/video-hdr-and-shaders.mdx
@@ -38,6 +38,45 @@ Anime4K is most useful for compatible animated sources at lower resolutions. It
 - Ambient GLOW and FRAME_EXTEND modes render around the video with configurable blur, color, warmth, vignette, and noise controls.
 - Automatic cropping can analyze supported videos for encoded black borders.
 
+### Tune GPU debanding
+
+| Control | Effect |
+| --- | --- |
+| Iterations | Repeats the debanding pass; higher values increase GPU cost. |
+| Threshold | Controls how readily subtle gradients are treated as banding. |
+| Range | Expands the neighborhood sampled around each pixel. |
+| Grain | Adds controlled noise to mask remaining quantization steps. |
+
+Begin with one iteration and conservative threshold/range. Too much processing
+can erase intentional texture or add visible noise.
+
+### Ambient modes
+
+**GLOW** extends color and light beyond the picture, while **FRAME_EXTEND**
+uses the frame itself as the surrounding composition. Settings include sample
+count, blur, intensity, saturation, warmth, vignette, and dither/noise.
+
+mpvRx caches compiled shader configurations and reduces the effective sample
+budget under thermal pressure. Adjust one parameter at a time; sample count and
+blur radius usually have the largest performance impact.
+
+### Filter presets
+
+Presets are creative starting points rather than calibration profiles. Preview
+them on representative bright, dark, animated, and skin-tone scenes before
+applying one broadly. Deep Black can crush shadow detail on an already dark
+master; vivid or neon looks can clip saturated colors.
+
+## Automatic crop
+
+Auto Crop samples several frames and looks for persistent encoded borders. It
+is conservative around dark scenes, short files, rotation, and changing aspect
+ratios, then caches a successful result for repeat playback.
+
+Disable it for intentional letterboxing, rapidly changing aspect ratios, or
+content where edge darkness is part of the image. Crop, stretch, pan, zoom, and
+ambient output share the final video geometry.
+
 ## Watch thermal behavior
 
-The thermal monitor samples headroom during playback. Shader budgets and Anime4K choices can be reduced before sustained heat becomes dropped frames. Use the statistics page to compare FPS, dropped frames, decoder state, and power draw after each change.
+The thermal monitor samples headroom during playback. Shader budgets and Anime4K choices can be reduced before sustained heat becomes dropped frames. Use [Statistics and refresh rate](/docs/playback/statistics-and-refresh-rate) to compare FPS, dropped frames, decoder state, temperature, and power draw after each change.
